# CLAUDE.md — Creative Coding Rules

Put this file in the root of the repo. Claude reads it before every task.

## Core Rule

Never ship the generic version. Before writing any code, decide on ONE bold idea (a concept, a mood, a signature detail) and build everything around it. If the result looks like something a thousand other projects already have, throw it away and start again.

## Banned Defaults

- Purple-to-blue gradients on white, centered hero + three feature cards, stock card grids
- Inter / Roboto / Arial / system fonts as the "design choice"
- Default shadows, default rounded corners, default Bootstrap/Tailwind look
- Lorem ipsum, placeholder names, grey boxes standing in for art
- Copy-pasting the same layout, palette, or animation across projects
- Dumping everything into one giant file with no structure

## Before You Code

1. Pick a direction in one line (e.g. "neon arcade," "paper cut-out," "dark cinematic," "hand-drawn chaos").
2. Choose a palette of 3-5 colors with one sharp accent. Define them as CSS variables / constants.
3. Choose 1 display font + 1 body font with real personality. Include a fallback stack.
4. Decide the signature moment: the one animation or interaction people will remember.
5. Then write code.

## Websites

- Strong visual hierarchy: huge type next to tiny type, tension between dense and empty space.
- Break the grid on purpose: overlap, rotate, offset, layer.
- Backgrounds with depth: gradients, grain, patterns, glow, blur, layered shapes. Never a flat color by default.
- Every section gets its own layout rhythm. No two sections should feel identical.
- Hover, focus, and scroll states must feel alive: micro-interactions on every button and link.
- Fully responsive (mobile first). Support RTL when the content is Persian/Arabic, with proper fonts and mirrored layout.
- Accessible: real contrast, keyboard focus, `prefers-reduced-motion` respected.

## Animation

- Motion has a purpose: guide the eye, give weight, create personality.
- Use easing with character (spring, overshoot, anticipation). Never plain `linear` or default `ease` unless intentional.
- Stagger and choreograph: elements enter in sequence, not all at once.
- One orchestrated page-load or scene-intro beats ten scattered effects.
- Layer techniques: CSS keyframes, SVG, canvas, WebGL/Three.js, scroll-linked motion. Choose what fits the idea.
- Keep it smooth: animate `transform` and `opacity`, avoid layout thrash, target 60fps.

## Skins (characters, textures, themes, UI skins)

- Give every skin a clear identity: silhouette, color story, one signature detail.
- Strong readable shapes first, detail second. It must read well at small size.
- Consistent style across a set, but each item clearly different from the others.
- Build a limited palette, then add highlights, shadows, and accents with intention.
- No recolors passed off as new skins. Change shape, pattern, or concept, not just hue.
- Name skins with personality.

## Code Quality

- Clean structure: separate HTML / CSS / JS (or components), clear names, short functions.
- Config at the top: colors, speeds, sizes, text. Easy to tweak without digging.
- Comments only where the idea is not obvious.
- No dead code, no unused libraries, no half-finished features.
- It must run on the first try. Test mentally before delivering.

## Working Style

- Do the work. Don't write long explanations unless asked.
- Don't ask a pile of questions. Make a strong creative choice and build it.
- Be ambitious: if a simple version and a striking version are both possible, build the striking one.
- After finishing, do one polish pass: spacing, timing, details, edge cases.
- Reply in the language the user writes in.

## Final Check

Before delivering, ask:
- Would someone remember this a day later?
- Does it have a point of view, or does it look like every template?
- Is there one detail that makes it feel handmade?

If any answer is no, push further.
